# infra/versions.env - the single file naming every pinned tool version, chart version, image
# digest and default model string for this project (docs/03-tech-stack.md section 5).
#
# Contract, established at M0-T0.4 and binding thereafter:
#
# 1. Values only. No logic, no command substitution, no shell expansion, no conditionals, and no
#    secrets. Every line is a plain key and value pair, read by `make up`, by the runner and by
#    CI. Secrets belong in .env (gitignored) or in Kubernetes Secrets, never in this file.
#
# 2. Every key stays present even when its value is empty. The key set is an M0 interface
#    contract: consumers may rely on a key existing before anyone has decided what it holds.
#    Empty values are correct and expected before M1, which is the milestone that populates the
#    cluster, application and observability pins.
#
# 3. Adding or removing a key after the v1.0 tag requires a hotfix ADR (AGENTS.md iron rule 6).
#    Before that tag, key changes are ordinary work but still land with the milestone that needs
#    them, together with the ADR that justifies the component. The M10 remediation re-opens this
#    file before the new v2.0 instrument freeze (ADR-136): CRYPTOGRAPHY_VERSION lands under ADR-130
#    with the Guardian group below. After v2.0, a key change is an instrument change that halts the
#    campaign and resets the pilot ladder, exactly as it does after v1.0.
#
# 4. No seeds, run counts, token lifetimes, budgets or thresholds belong in this file. Those are
#    owned by docs/protocols/evaluation-protocol.md and docs/protocols/security-policy.md and are
#    single-homed there per .agents/rules/00-source-of-truth.md. This file pins the tooling; it
#    never parameterises the experiment.
#
# 5. Hash contract. This file is digested by scripts/versions_env_sha256.py, which normalises
#    CRLF line endings to LF before hashing, so the digest is identical on a Windows checkout and
#    inside WSL2. That digest is the `versions_env_sha256` field of every run manifest
#    (docs/protocols/data-management.md section 2). Do not hash this file with sha256sum or any
#    other tool that skips the normalisation step: an un-normalised digest records the host
#    rather than the stack, which breaks NFR1 and traceability row T-15.
#
# Groups follow the layer table in docs/03-tech-stack.md section 2; the ADR named on each group
# is the authority for what that layer contains and why.

# --- Cluster (ADR-001) ---
KIND_VERSION=v0.32.0
KIND_NODE_IMAGE=kindest/node:v1.36.1@sha256:3489c7674813ba5d8b1a9977baea8a6e553784dab7b84759d1014dbd78f7ebd5
CILIUM_CHART_VERSION=1.20.1
CILIUM_CLI_VERSION=v0.19.7
KUSTOMIZE_VERSION=v5.8.1
KUBECTL_VERSION=v1.36.4
HELM_VERSION=v4.2.4

# --- Target application: Online Boutique, plus the standalone Locust load driver (ADR-001) ---
# This group's heading changed at M1-T1.2 and the change is not cosmetic. It read "plus its
# bundled Locust load generator", but infra/boutique/remove-loadgenerator.yaml deletes the
# upstream loadgenerator Deployment: left in, it drives uncontrolled background traffic in every
# run, which corrupts the RQ4 interference measure (evaluation-protocol section 3) and makes
# load_profile_id a fiction. LOCUST_IMAGE_DIGEST therefore pins the standalone locustio/locust
# image that infra/locust/deployment.yaml runs idle and a run drives per load profile -- not the
# image the deleted workload used. ADR-015.
BOUTIQUE_MANIFEST_REF=v0.10.6
BOUTIQUE_IMAGE_DIGESTS_FILE=infra/boutique/images.yaml
LOCUST_IMAGE_DIGEST=sha256:0d7e43081c5f9437aed6513dc5827ec6984f024d00141a201a5b6be6e9a63e5d

# --- Monitor: runtime, metric and image signal sources (ADR-007) ---
# The fourth signal family, the Kubernetes audit stream, is a kube-apiserver feature configured
# in infra/kind-config.yaml rather than a separately versioned component, so it has no key here.
# Phase B pins were resolved from upstream chart indexes and the container registry on 2026-08-27,
# then exercised by the repository's static render tests. FALCOSIDEKICK_VERSION is the subchart
# version locked by the selected Falco chart's Chart.lock, not an independently installed release.
FALCO_CHART_VERSION=8.0.1
FALCO_DRIVER=modern_ebpf
FALCOSIDEKICK_VERSION=0.12.1
PROMETHEUS_CHART_VERSION=29.27.0
ALERTMANAGER_VERSION=1.35.0
TRIVY_VERSION=0.69.3

# --- Evidence aggregator (ADR-007, ADR-021, ADR-046) ---
# The base digest is the already verified Python 3.12 slim-bookworm manifest-list choice used by
# the Guardian image in this same file. The separate key records which build consumes it and lets a
# later component move without silently moving the other. The local image name is loaded into kind.
MONITOR_PYTHON_BASE_IMAGE=python:3.12-slim-bookworm@sha256:0f5b26b9518d002b6173fd61daad821fa340635ebfec5bba471013f9ca114579
MONITOR_IMAGE=agentic-overwatch/evidence-aggregator:local
# PYDANTIC_VERSION is a runtime dependency of the evaluated artefact, not development tooling, and
# that is why it lives here rather than in pyproject.toml. It validates every EvidenceEvent the
# campaign produces, so a version change is a change to the software under study and belongs in the
# versions_env_sha256 digest every sealed run bundle carries. ADR-021 records the decision and
# separates it from the CI *tool* pins -- ruff and pytest -- that ADR-011 deliberately keeps as
# literals in the workflow. scripts/install_runtime_deps.sh installs from this key, and CI calls
# that same script, so the pin has exactly one reader.
PYDANTIC_VERSION=2.13.4

# --- Agents, gateway and inference (ADR-002, ADR-005) ---
# PYTHON_VERSION is the one interpreter version for every Python component in the repo:
# aggregator, guardian, agents, runner and analysis. It is pinned to the MINOR version rather than
# to a patch, deliberately: the patch level is a property of whichever image or distribution
# provides the interpreter -- WSL2 Ubuntu-24.04 ships 3.12.3, ubuntu-latest ships whatever its
# image was built with -- and a patch pin no runner can honour is a pin that would be either
# ignored or lied about. The minor version is the real interface: it is what pyproject.toml's
# requires-python and ruff's target-version already range over, and what a syntax or stdlib
# difference would actually turn on.
PYTHON_VERSION=3.12
LANGGRAPH_VERSION=1.2.11
LITELLM_VERSION=1.98.0
# Both are runtime dependencies of the evaluated artefact on the same argument the Guardian group
# below makes for FastAPI and PyJWT: LangGraph is the topology the three structure-ablation arms
# ARE (ADR-002), and LiteLLM is the single call surface every arm reaches a model through
# (ADR-005). A version change to either changes the software the RQ results describe, so it belongs
# in the versions_env_sha256 digest every sealed bundle carries. scripts/install_runtime_deps.sh
# installs both from these keys under the `agents` component, so each pin has exactly one reader.
#
# MODEL_ALIAS is the stable internal name every call under agents/ addresses. ADR-005 fixes it by
# name -- "a stable internal alias (`overwatch-primary`)" -- and the alias is the entire mechanism
# by which provider churn is absorbed without touching a graph, a prompt or a test. Nothing in
# agents/ ever names a provider model; it names this alias, and the key below is the one place the
# alias resolves.
MODEL_ALIAS=overwatch-primary
# MODEL_PROVIDER_STRING holds the selected DEV/PILOT model. The dev/pilot endpoint is
# OpenAI-compatible, so LiteLLM's custom OpenAI-compatible provider prefix is part of the
# provider string.
# Terra is the tracked primary; use MODEL_PROVIDER_STRING=custom_openai/gpt-5.6-luna as a per-run
# override when Luna is intentionally selected. GATE records the exact answered model.
MODEL_PROVIDER_STRING=custom_openai/gpt-5.6-terra
# PARITY_TOKENIZER names the fixed reference tokenizer `make parity-check` counts prompt tokens
# with. It is a TOOL PIN, not an experiment parameter, so rule 4 above is satisfied rather than
# bent: every tolerance, cap and budget the parity check compares its counts AGAINST is single-homed
# in docs/protocols/prompt-parity-policy.md, and this key says only which counter produced the
# numbers. Changing it changes no threshold; it changes the instrument, which is what this file
# exists to pin.
#
# It is pinned rather than derived from MODEL_PROVIDER_STRING because the parity gate is
# CI-blocking and must reproduce on a runner with no provider credential and no network, and
# because a parity number that moves when a free catalogue churns is not a parity number. The
# campaign model's own tokenizer is compared against this one ONCE, at GATE, alongside R-01's limit
# re-verification, and reported as a cross-check -- never as the gate.
#
# agents/registry/tokenizer.py carries the same string as a module constant and agents/tests/
# asserts the two are equal. That is ADR-022's rule for a copy: legal when a test makes "copy" mean
# "copy that cannot drift". A tokenizer identity that appears in an emitted token table and in
# every bundle manifest cannot be read from a shell-format file at import time without giving this
# repository a second .env parser, so the constant is mirrored and the mirror is checked.
PARITY_TOKENIZER=overwatch-ref-1
# The self-host inference fallback is named as "Ollama / vLLM" by docs/03-tech-stack.md section 2,
# so both runtimes carry a key. Section 4 currently plans the Ollama path; the other key stays
# present and empty until that choice is revisited, per rule 2 above.
OLLAMA_VERSION=
VLLM_VERSION=

# --- Guardian (ADR-003, ADR-004, ADR-025) ---
# All three are runtime dependencies of the evaluated artefact, not development tooling, which is
# why they are pinned here rather than in pyproject.toml -- the distinction ADR-011 draws and
# ADR-021 applied to the aggregator's pydantic. FastAPI serves the mediation surface every agent
# action passes through, PyJWT verifies every capability token, and OPA decides every permit and
# deny. A change to any of them is a change to the enforcement path the security results describe,
# so it belongs in the versions_env_sha256 digest every sealed run bundle carries.
#
# OPA_VERSION is a binary rather than a Python package, and scripts/guardian_test.sh acquires it
# with a checksum check rather than trusting PATH. That keeps `make guardian-test` self-contained
# and keeps docs/04-repo-conventions.md section 4's job assignment true: the hygiene job installs
# nothing of its own.
#
# KUBERNETES_CLIENT_VERSION joins them at M4, the milestone that first assembles a Guardian able to
# execute rather than only decide: the cluster executor behind every permitted action reaches the
# API server through this client. It is the same class as the three above -- a runtime dependency of
# the evaluated artefact, not development tooling -- so it is pinned here rather than in
# pyproject.toml, exactly as FastAPI and PyJWT are. It is also a pin that may appear in exactly one
# component: guardian/ is the sole credential holder, and .agents/rules/10-guardian-first.md forbids
# a Kubernetes client under agents/ at all, which scripts/grep_guard.sh checks.
#
# The client's major version tracks the Kubernetes minor it is generated from, so 36.x is the client
# for the 1.36 API server that KIND_NODE_IMAGE and KUBECTL_VERSION already pin at the top of this
# file. Those three move together or not at all; a client whose major has drifted from the cluster
# is the failure this pin exists to make visible in review rather than at runtime.
FASTAPI_VERSION=0.141.1
OPA_VERSION=v1.19.1
# ADR-182: 2.13.0 carries CVE-2026-102268 (CRITICAL), fixed in 2.14.0. PyJWT verifies every
# capability token the Guardian issues and every one it is presented, so this is on the
# authenticated path rather than an incidental dependency.
PYJWT_VERSION=2.14.0
# ADR-182: the OS package the pinned base image's own layer still ships short of. The base digest
# stays put -- the moving python:3.12-slim-bookworm tag has switched to Ubuntu noble, and the
# bookworm-pinned 3.12.11 image would downgrade the interpreter from 3.12.14 -- and this one
# targeted, version-pinned upgrade moves perl-base 5.36.0-7+deb12u3 to 7+deb12u4, which is where
# Debian fixed CVE-2026-8376, CVE-2026-13221 and CVE-2026-42496. Pinned to the exact version
# rather than "latest security" so the built image is reproducible from this file and a future
# archive that drops the version fails the build visibly instead of silently changing the image.
PERL_BASE_VERSION=5.36.0-7+deb12u4
# The audit chain's Ed25519 record signature (ADR-130, M10). A runtime dependency of the evaluated
# Guardian artefact — the signing and verification path is what makes a whole-chain rewrite
# detectable — so it is pinned here, single-homed, on the same argument PYJWT and the client below
# carry, and header rule 3 requires the ADR. The key is Ed25519 through `cryptography` directly and
# is distinct from the HS256 token key; token verification stays on PYJWT.
CRYPTOGRAPHY_VERSION=44.0.0
KUBERNETES_CLIENT_VERSION=36.0.3
# Guardian's ASGI server and build base are deployment inputs. The base is a manifest-list digest so
# a local kind build resolves the correct architecture while remaining immutable.
UVICORN_VERSION=0.52.4
GUARDIAN_PYTHON_BASE_IMAGE=python:3.12-slim-bookworm@sha256:0f5b26b9518d002b6173fd61daad821fa340635ebfec5bba471013f9ca114579
GUARDIAN_IMAGE=agentic-overwatch/guardian:local

# --- Injection: fault injection and vulnerable images ---
# CVE_IMAGE_DIGESTS_FILE follows BOUTIQUE_IMAGE_DIGESTS_FILE above rather than inventing a second
# convention, and for the reason rule 1 gives: this file is values only, so a set of digests is
# named from here and lives in the file named. scenarios/cve/images.yaml is that file. Its entries
# carry an empty `digest` today, which is rule 2 one level down -- the entry set is the interface
# contract, and the digest is what M5's Tier B fills when a network exists to resolve it against.
# Inventing a digest instead would put an unverifiable value inside versions_env_sha256, which
# every sealed run bundle carries and nothing in this repository could ever check; an empty field
# is visible and is refused by `sh scripts/cve_mirror.sh --verify`, which fails closed until then.
#
# CHAOS_MESH_CHART_VERSION stays empty for the same reason, at the level above. A chart version is
# resolved against the live chart repository -- it is that repository's answer to "what releases
# exist" -- and M5's Tier A has no network. This key therefore follows FALCO_CHART_VERSION in the
# Monitor group above, which M2 left empty through the whole of its offline Phase A and fills in
# its own cluster-dependent Phase B; a chart pin written from memory is a pin nobody verified, and
# it would be indistinguishable in this file from one that was.
#
# Nothing offline depends on the chart pin, which is what makes leaving it empty safe rather than
# merely honest. The six CRDs under scenarios/chaosmesh/ pin `apiVersion: chaos-mesh.org/v1alpha1`,
# and that API version -- not the chart that installs the controller -- is the contract those
# documents are actually written against. A chart bump that keeps v1alpha1 leaves every one of them
# valid; one that did not would break them whatever number stood here.
CHAOS_MESH_CHART_VERSION=2.8.4
CVE_IMAGE_DIGESTS_FILE=scenarios/cve/images.yaml

# --- Observability (ADR-006 for Langfuse and OTel; ADR-013 for the metrics trio) ---
# GRAFANA_VERSION, KUBE_STATE_METRICS_VERSION and PROMETHEUS_CHART_VERSION are three separate
# chart pins rather than one umbrella pin, which is the whole argument of ADR-013: rule 2 above
# makes this key set an interface contract, and kube-prometheus-stack would give one version
# that cannot honestly populate three keys. node-exporter is installed by the Prometheus chart
# and takes no key of its own, the same treatment the Kubernetes audit stream gets in the
# Monitor group above and for the same reason -- it is not separately versioned here.
# GRAFANA_VERSION pins grafana/grafana, which upstream marked deprecated when the chart moved to
# the grafana-community repository. The pin still resolves and the artefact is frozen at v1.0, so
# reproducibility is unaffected; ADR-013 records the deprecation, the successor repository and
# the evidence that infra/observability/grafana-values.yaml renders identically on both.
#
# LANGFUSE_VERSION pins the self-hosted `langfuse/langfuse` v3 image tag, and with it the matching
# `langfuse/langfuse-worker` tag: v3 is a two-container application shipped from one release, so one
# key is one version rather than two homes for it. infra/observability/langfuse/docker-compose.yml
# reads it and restates no version literal of its own -- the compose stack is invoked with THIS file
# as an --env-file, so the trace plane is pinned from the same surface as every other component.
# v3 rather than the current v4 line, deliberately: ADR-006 chose Langfuse at the v3 self-host shape
# and the artefact freezes at v1.0, so following a major upstream while the campaign is unrun would
# change the instrument mid-study for no result it could affect.
#
# LANGFUSE_STACK_IMAGES_FILE follows BOUTIQUE_IMAGE_DIGESTS_FILE above rather than inventing a
# second convention: a component whose install needs several image pins names the file that holds
# them, and that file is reachable from here by exactly one path, so this file's claim to be the
# single pinning surface stays true. Langfuse v3 requires Postgres, ClickHouse, Redis and MinIO
# beside it; those four are pins of the trace plane's own dependencies rather than layers of the
# stack docs/03-tech-stack.md section 2 tabulates, which is why they sit next to the compose file
# they serve instead of becoming four groups' worth of keys here. Upstream's own compose leaves
# three of the four floating on a moving tag; the file this key names does not.
LANGFUSE_VERSION=3.225.5
LANGFUSE_STACK_IMAGES_FILE=infra/observability/langfuse/images.env
# OTEL_COLLECTOR_VERSION pins otel/opentelemetry-collector-contrib, the Tier B hop between the
# agents' OTLP exporter and Langfuse (infra/observability/otel/). Tier A writes spans to a file
# exporter and needs no collector at all, which is why this pin gates nothing offline.
#
# OTEL_PYTHON_SDK_VERSION is ONE key covering BOTH `opentelemetry-sdk` and
# `opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http`, and that is deliberate rather than lazy. The two are
# released in lockstep from one repository under one version number -- the exporter's own metadata
# requires the SDK at the same version -- so two keys would be two homes for one version, which is
# the defect rule 2's interface contract and ADR-013's three-chart argument both exist to prevent.
# The difference from ADR-013 is the direction: there, one umbrella version could not honestly
# populate three keys, so three keys; here, one version genuinely covers two distributions, so one
# key. scripts/install_runtime_deps.sh reads it twice, under the `agents` component, and installs
# both distributions at it.
OTEL_COLLECTOR_VERSION=0.159.0
OTEL_PYTHON_SDK_VERSION=1.44.0
GRAFANA_VERSION=10.5.15
KUBE_STATE_METRICS_VERSION=8.4.0

# --- Analysis (ADR-021, ADR-043) ---
# The three runtime dependencies of analysis/stats, pinned at M6. pandas organises the raw run
# bundles into frames; statsmodels computes the Wilson score interval evaluation-protocol §3 and
# §3.5.6 require (proportion_confint method='wilson', Pearson's variant, never 'wilson_cc');
# matplotlib renders the figures the notebooks display. They are runtime dependencies of the
# evaluated artefact's analysis, not development tooling, so they live here and enter every run
# manifest's versions_env_sha256 -- a statsmodels version change is a change to how a reported
# interval is computed and belongs in the digest. scripts/install_runtime_deps.sh installs them
# under the `analysis` component; ci.yml's analysis pytest step is what exercises them.
PANDAS_VERSION=3.0.5
STATSMODELS_VERSION=0.15.0
MATPLOTLIB_VERSION=3.10.8
